    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "We often denote covariance by $ \\sigma_{x,y} $. Because $ E(x) = \\mu_{x} $ and $ E(y) = \\mu_{y} $, expansion of the product leads to further simplification,",
      "source_page": 53,
      "printed_page": 33,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p53:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        50.0,
        667.0,
        1008.0,
        750.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_chapter3_formula009]]",
      "source_page": 53,
      "printed_page": 33,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p53:b8"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        253.0,
        773.0,
        798.0,
        943.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In words, the covariance is the mean of the pairwise cross-product $x$ $y$ minus the cross-product of the means. The sampling estimator of $\\sigma(x,y)$ is similar in form to that for a variance,",
      "source_page": 53,
      "printed_page": 33,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p53:b10"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        42.0,
